L67: # Getting Started cite27†​ L68: ## Overview cite28†​ L69: 
L70: Vite (French word for "quick", pronounced `/viːt/`, like "veet") is a build tool that aims to provide a faster and leaner development experience for modern web projects. It consists of two major parts:
L71: 
L72:   * A dev server that provides cite3†rich feature enhancements over cite29†native ES modules†developer.mozilla.org , for example extremely fast cite30†Hot Module Replacement (HMR) .
L73:   * A build command that bundles your code with cite31†Rolldown†rolldown.rs , pre-configured to output highly optimized static assets for production.
L74: 
L75: Vite is opinionated and comes with sensible defaults out of the box. Read about what's possible in the cite3†Features Guide . Support for frameworks or integration with other tools is possible through cite5†Plugins . The cite20†Config Section explains how to adapt Vite to your project if needed.
L76: Vite is also highly extensible via its cite17†Plugin API and cite19†JavaScript API with full typing support.
L77: 
L78: You can learn more about the rationale behind the project in the cite2†Why Vite section.
L79: 
L80: cite32†Learn Vite through interactive tutorials on Scrimba†scrimba.com L81: ## Browser Support cite33†​ L82: During development, Vite assumes that a modern browser is used. This means the browser supports most of the latest JavaScript and CSS features. For that reason, Vite sets cite34†`esnext` as the transform target†oxc.rs . This prevents syntax lowering, letting Vite serve modules as close as possible to the original source code. Vite injects some runtime code to make the development server work.
L83: This code uses features included in cite35†Baseline†web-platform-dx.github.io Newly Available at the time of each major release (2026-01-01 for this major).
L84: For production builds, Vite by default targets cite35†Baseline†web-platform-dx.github.io Widely Available browser versions as of a date fixed for each major release. For this major, that corresponds to cite36†browser versions released around mid-2023†web-platform-dx.github.io . The target can be lowered via configuration. Additionally, legacy browsers can be supported via the official cite37†@vitejs/plugin-legacy†github.com . See the cite8†Building for Production section for more details.
L85: ## Trying Vite Online cite38†​ L86: 
L87: You can try Vite online on cite39†StackBlitz†vite.new . It runs the Vite-based build setup directly in the browser, so it is almost identical to the local setup but doesn't require installing anything on your machine. You can navigate to `vite.new/{template}` to select which framework to use.
L88: 
L89: The supported template presets are:
L90: JavaScript  | TypeScript
L91: --- | ---
L92: cite40†vanilla†vite.new | cite41†vanilla-ts†vite.new L93: cite42†vue†vite.new | cite43†vue-ts†vite.new L94: cite44†react†vite.new | cite45†react-ts†vite.new L95: cite46†preact†vite.new | cite47†preact-ts†vite.new L96: cite48†lit†vite.new | cite49†lit-ts†vite.new L97: cite50†svelte†vite.new | cite51†svelte-ts†vite.new L98: cite52†solid†vite.new | cite53†solid-ts†vite.new L99: cite54†qwik†vite.new | cite55†qwik-ts†vite.new L100: ## Scaffolding Your First Vite Project cite56†​ L101: 
L102: [Input]npm[Input]Yarn[Input]pnpm[Input]Bun[Input]Deno
L103: 
L104: bash
L105: 
L106:     `$ npm create vite@latest`
L107: 
L108: bash
L109: 
L110:     `$ yarn create vite`
L111: 
L112: bash
L113: 
L114:     `$ pnpm create vite`
L115: 
L116: bash
L117: 
L118:     `$ bun create vite`
L119: 
L120: bash
L121: 
L122:     `$ deno init --npm vite`
L123: 
L124: Then follow the prompts!
L125: 
L126: cite57†Watch an interactive lesson on Scrimba†scrimba.com L127: 
L128: Compatibility Note
L129: Vite requires cite58†Node.js†nodejs.org version 20.19+, 22.12+. However, some templates require a higher Node.js version to work, please upgrade if your package manager warns about it.
L130: 
L131: Using create vite with command line options
L132: 
L133: You can also directly specify the project name and the template you want to use via additional command line options. For example, to scaffold a Vite + Vue project, run:
L134: 
L135: [Input]npm[Input]Yarn[Input]pnpm[Input]Bun[Input]Deno
L136: bash
L137: 
L138:     `# npm 7+, extra double-dash is needed:
L139:     $ npm create vite@latest my-vue-app -- --template vue`
L140: 
L141: bash
L142: 
L143:     `$ yarn create vite my-vue-app --template vue`
L144: 
L145: bash
L146: 
L147:     `$ pnpm create vite my-vue-app --template vue`
L148: 
L149: bash
L150: 
L151:     `$ bun create vite my-vue-app --template vue`
L152: 
L153: bash
L154: 
L155:     `$ deno init --npm vite my-vue-app --template vue`
L156: See cite59†create-vite†github.com for more details on each supported template: `vanilla`, `vanilla-ts`, `vue`, `vue-ts`, `react`, `react-compiler`, `react-ts`, `react-compiler-ts`, `preact`, `preact-ts`, `lit`, `lit-ts`, `svelte`, `svelte-ts`, `solid`, `solid-ts`, `qwik`, `qwik-ts`.
L157: 
L158: You can use `.` for the project name to scaffold in the current directory.
L159: 
L160: To create a project without interactive prompts, you can use the `--no-interactive` flag.
L161: ## Community Templates cite60†​ L162: 
L163: create-vite is a tool to quickly start a project from a basic template for popular frameworks. Check out Awesome Vite for cite61†community maintained templates†github.com that include other tools or target different frameworks.
L164: 
L165: For a template at `https://github.com/user/project`, you can try it out online using `https://github.stackblitz.com/user/project` (adding `.stackblitz` after `github` to the URL of the project).
L166: You can also use a tool like cite62†tiged†github.com to scaffold your project with one of the templates. Assuming the project is on GitHub and uses `main` as the default branch, you can create a local copy using:
L167: 
L168: bash
L169: 
L170:     `npx tiged user/project my-project
L171:     cd my-project
L172: 
L173:     npm install
L174:     npm run dev`
L175: ## Manual Installation cite63†​ L176: 
L177: In your project, you can install the `vite` CLI using:
L178: 
L179: [Input]npm[Input]Yarn[Input]pnpm[Input]Bun[Input]Deno
L180: 
L181: bash
L182: 
L183:     `$ npm install -D vite`
L184: 
L185: bash
L186: 
L187:     `$ yarn add -D vite`
L188: 
L189: bash
L190: 
L191:     `$ pnpm add -D vite`
L192: 
L193: bash
L194: 
L195:     `$ bun add -D vite`
L196: 
L197: bash
L198: 
L199:     `$ deno add -D npm:vite`
L200: 
L201: And create an `index.html` file like this:
L202: 
L203: html
L204: 
L205:     `<p>Hello Vite!</p>`
L206: 
L207: Then run the appropriate CLI command in your terminal:
L208: [Input]npm[Input]Yarn[Input]pnpm[Input]Bun[Input]Deno
L209: 
L210: bash
L211: 
L212:     `$ npx vite`
L213: 
L214: bash
L215: 
L216:     `$ yarn vite`
L217: 
L218: bash
L219: 
L220:     `$ pnpm vite`
L221: 
L222: bash
L223: 
L224:     `$ bunx vite`
L225: 
L226: bash
L227: 
L228:     `$ deno run -A npm:vite`
L229: 
L230: The `index.html` will be served on `http://localhost:5173`.
